# Working on {{ github_runner_host_name }}

You are running as a GitHub Actions job on {{ github_runner_host_name }}, {{ github_runner_host_description }}, working in
a checkout of {{ item.repo }}: as the lead the workflow starts, or as one of
the roles in `~/.claude/agents/` the lead calls. These rules hold for all of
you, in every repository here, on top of the repository's own CLAUDE.md.
The lead has rules of its own besides, in its system prompt.

## Where you are

- **Nothing you leave here survives the job.** Every job gets a fresh
  container, removed when the job ends, checkout included. The preview is
  the one thing that stays, and it is not here: it runs on the executor,
  from what you send it.
- **There is no root.** `apt-get` and `sudo` do not work. Use the toolchains
  the workflow sets up, or tell the user in a comment what the image lacks.
- **`uv` and `docker` are installed.** Use `uv` for Python: `uv sync`,
  `uv run pytest`, `uvx <tool>`, and `uv python install` when the
  repository wants a Python the image lacks. `docker` and `docker-compose`
  are the podman on this box, through `DOCKER_HOST`, so what you start
  outlives the job: start containers with `docker run --rm`, for the
  tests' services only; the preview is the executor's, below. Your
  checkout is at the same path on the host, so a compose file's bind
  mounts of it work.
- **docker-compose refuses `start_interval` here.** This box's podman
  reports Docker API 1.41, and compose will not start a file whose health
  check sets `start_interval` ("feature require Docker Engine v25 or
  later"). Never take it out of the repository's files: it is a limit of
  this box. The executor resets it in a stack by itself; for every compose
  run of your own, write `$RUNNER_TEMP/no-start-interval.yaml`, outside the
  checkout so nothing commits it, with `healthcheck: start_interval: !reset null`
  under each service that sets it, and add it last:
  `docker-compose -f compose.yaml -f "$RUNNER_TEMP/no-start-interval.yaml" up …`.
  A script or `make` target that names its compose files itself, an e2e
  script among them, you run as its steps, with the file added to each
  compose call, and the PR says it was run that way.
- **A repository's dev containers are yours to run, and its plays on them,**
  through its own `make` targets, privileged systemd containers included:
  they are only as privileged as this box's runner account, and fenced as
  you are. Its `.env`, which on the user's workstation names their keys,
  you write from `.env.example` with throwaway values: a key from
  `ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N '' -f "$RUNNER_TEMP/dev-key"`, and `/dev/null`
  for a file the dev containers do not read, such as `known_hosts`. Never
  under `/tmp`, which the containers you start cannot see. Take them down
  with the repository's own target or `docker-compose down -v` before your
  turn ends; they would otherwise hold memory the box's other runners need.
- **The network is the internet only.** Every port on this box outside this
  repository's own, and every address on its LAN and private network, is
  firewalled off for you and for everything you start. A refused connection
  to one of them is the firewall doing its job, not a bug to work around.
  The one exception is the GPU model server's API and manifest on the
  host's private network, and only a repository that has the `gpu-models` skill, and so
  the key in its environment, has any use for them.
- **What you start is reached through the host, on your own ports.** A
  container you start is at `host.containers.internal:<port>`, on one of
  the ports under "Your ports". `localhost` is your own container, any other
  port is firewalled but the stack's, and a port published on `127.0.0.1`,
  as many compose files do, refuses you even in your range, so publish on
  all addresses. When tests need a service and the stack is up, use the
  stack's rather than starting another. Tests that still could not run are
  named in the PR with the exact error.
- **Run one plain command per call.** The permissions here allow commands
  by their first word, and refuse what they cannot read that way: a `for`
  or `while` loop, a variable set and used (`url=...; curl $url`), a
  command's output in another's arguments (`$(...)`), and a command
  with a variable in front of it: write `env APP_X=1 bun test`, never
  `APP_X=1 bun test`. Reading is allowed in the checkout,
  the work folder, `/tmp`, `~/.claude` and `/usr/local/bin`, and refused
  anywhere else. Every refusal costs a turn and a retry. Write values out
  instead of in variables, run a loop's commands one by one, and prefer
  absolute paths and `git -C <dir>` to `cd`.
- **Scratch files go under `/tmp`**, never in the checkout.
- **A related repository sits beside the checkout.** When the lead's prompt
  names related repositories, `claude-job-clone <name>` clones one to
  `../<name>`, and only those; do it when the work needs that repository,
  not before. Claude Code does not load its CLAUDE.md for you, so read
  `../<name>/CLAUDE.md` first and follow it there. Run its own tests, and
  use `git -C <path>`, since every rule here holds there too, the default
  branch's above all. A stack is the checkout's HEAD alone, so a preview
  that needs a related repository's change cannot have it: say so in the
  PR.

## What only the user does

- **Never push to the default branch.** Work on your own branch and open a
  PR, also for a one-line fix. Your token can push to it, and on GitHub's
  free plan nothing on GitHub's side stops it, so this rule is what does.
- **Never merge a PR**, yours or anyone's, and never approve or mark ready
  someone else's. Merging is how the user accepts the work.
- **Never rewrite what the user approved.** Commits up to the PR's latest
  approving review are fixed, unless the user asks in a comment to squash
  them. Force push only your own PR branch, and only
  with `--force-with-lease`. Never delete branches or tags you did not
  create.
- **Workflow files are the user's to push.** Nothing under
  `.github/workflows/` goes in a commit, new files included: your token
  cannot push it, and GitHub refuses the whole push. When the work needs a
  workflow changed, write the change as a unified diff under `/tmp`, never
  in the checkout, and give it to the lead, which proposes it in the PR.
- **Never change what guards the repository**: branch rules, the
  repository's settings, or its workflows' triggers and permissions. If one
  of them is in your way, say so in a comment.

## What GitHub may see of this box

Nothing you write on GitHub, a comment, the PR, an issue or a commit
message, names this box's insides: no paths outside the checkout, no
account, unit or runner names, no ports but the preview's own. Say "the
preview", "the runner" or "the test service" instead. The preview's URL
and its phone page are the exception, since they are how the user opens it.

## Tests

A change in behaviour comes with a test that fails without it: a feature,
a fixed bug, a changed rule. A change no test could tell apart needs none:
documentation, a CLAUDE.md, comments, wording no test checks, a refactor
the existing tests already cover. Never add a test only to have one. The
PR says which it was, under its summary: the tests added, or
"No new tests: <why>", with no counts or results. Whenever the change touches code, the whole suite
passes before you push.

**A repository's hooks hold too.** With a `.pre-commit-config.yaml`, run
`prek run --all-files` before you push, as `uv run prek` when the repository
depends on it. That leaves out the commit message rules, which the job's
`commit-msg` hook runs at each commit: a commit it refuses is reworded, never
sent with `--no-verify`. The types a repository allows are in its commit
rules, such as `.gitlint`, not in your habits.

**On a PR, the Claude settings in the checkout are the default branch's.** The
action puts back `CLAUDE.md`, `CLAUDE.local.md`, `.claude`, `.husky`,
`.gitmodules`, `.mcp.json`, `.claude.json` and `.ripgreprc` from it before the
job starts, so they show as changed, and committing them undoes the PR's own
changes to them. Stage files by name, never with `git add -A`, `git add .` or
`git commit -a`, and leave those out. To change one, take the PR's version
first with `git restore --source=HEAD --staged --worktree -- <path>`. What the
job leaves uncommitted in them is not saved.

